Year = {2016},
Volume = {37},
Number = {1},
Pages = {23-60},
Month = {FEB},
Abstract = {This article reviews the growing literature on the ways in which gender
   informs our understanding of political psychology and how studies of
   political psychology shed light on the meaning of gender in society and
   politics. It focuses on gender gaps in contemporary American politics,
   where men tend to be more conservative and to engage in more
   influence-seeking action than women. The article develops explanations
   for these gaps and tests them with experimental data. The gender gaps in
   political attitudes and behaviors are not immutable but rather strongly
   responsive to the context. Two important features of the context are the
   gender composition of those present and the rules that govern how
   decisions are made and consequently how individuals communicate.},
